      {
        "thread_id": "TH2",
        "thread_goal": "Draft and refine the standard loading sequence and safety checks",
        "messages": [
          {
            "msg_id": "M19",
            "speaker_id": 36844,
            "reply_to": null,
            "text": "Ok, here is a first draft of the standard loading sequence for T2. Let me know what you think. I tried to cover the main cargo types.",
            "mentions": [
              77364,
              54508,
              81581,
              205041
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T2"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M20",
            "speaker_id": 81581,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "Thanks @36844. This is a solid start. I have a few suggestions. For mixed cargo, we should explicitly state 'heaviest and least fragile items on the bottom, centered over the axle'. Also, we need a step for checking weight distribution not just at the end, but halfway through the load. This prevents having to rework the whole container.",
            "mentions": [
              36844
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "bulb",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M21",
            "speaker_id": 205041,
            "reply_to": "M20",
            "text": "What about really tall pallets? Where do they go?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M22",
            "speaker_id": 81581,
            "reply_to": "M21",
            "text": "Good question. Tall but light items should go last, secured with straps to the container walls to prevent tipping. We should add that to the sequence and also make sure it's in the visual guide A4 later on.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A4"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M23",
            "speaker_id": 77364,
            "reply_to": "M20",
            "text": "I remember we used to have a rule about always loading from the front to the back. Is that still a thing?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M24",
            "speaker_id": 36844,
            "reply_to": "M23",
            "text": "It depends on the trailer type. For most of our current fleet, balancing the load over the axles is more important than just front-to-back. That's what the new sequence tries to capture.",
            "mentions": [
              77364
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M25",
            "speaker_id": 81581,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "This also connects directly to the safety checklist T3. Before any loading starts, we need to confirm the cargo type and have a rough plan. I think the first three checks on A2 should be: 1. Area clear of debris. 2. Correct PPE worn by all crew. 3. Cargo matches manifest and a loading plan is discussed. What do you all think?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T3",
              "A2"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D4"
            ],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "safety_vest",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M26",
            "speaker_id": 54508,
            "reply_to": "M25",
            "text": "That makes sense. A quick 30-second huddle before starting can prevent a lot of mistakes. The check for 'equipment condition' should also be on that mandatory list.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M27",
            "speaker_id": 81581,
            "reply_to": "M26",
            "text": "Good add. So the mandatory pre-start checks are: Area Clear, Correct PPE, Cargo/Plan Match, and Equipment Condition. Agreed?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D4"
            ],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M28",
            "speaker_id": 36844,
            "reply_to": "M27",
            "text": "Yes, agreed.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "+1",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M29",
            "speaker_id": 205041,
            "reply_to": "M27",
            "text": "I agree.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "+1",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M30",
            "speaker_id": 77364,
            "reply_to": "M27",
            "text": "Okay, that's decided for the safety checks.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D4"
            ],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M31",
            "speaker_id": 81581,
            "reply_to": "M30",
            "text": "Great. Now back to the loading sequence. Based on the feedback, I've drafted a revised flow. See below. If we're happy with this, we can lock it in as the sequence for the first version of the SOP A1.\n1. Stage cargo & equipment.\n2. Plan load with crew, confirming weight distribution.\n3. Load heaviest items over axles.\n4. Build up layers, distributing weight evenly.\n5. Perform mid-load stability check.\n6. Place light/tall items last.\n7. Secure all cargo with straps/bars.\n8. Final check and photo documentation.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A1"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D3"
            ],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M32",
            "speaker_id": 36844,
            "reply_to": "M31",
            "text": "This looks much better. It's clear and covers the points we discussed. I'm good with this.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M33",
            "speaker_id": 54508,
            "reply_to": "M31",
            "text": "The photo documentation is a great idea for liability.",
            "mentions": [],
